D={
"hittite-empire":dict(government="The Hittite great king ruled from Hattusa with a council of nobles (the panku), managed vassal kings by written treaties sworn before gods, and had a law code with fines rather than mutilation for many crimes.",architecture="Hattusa had 6 km of walls with the Lion Gate, the Yazılıkaya rock sanctuary with carved gods, and huge granaries.",technology="Early iron working, light three-man chariots, and archives of thousands of cuneiform tablets in several languages.",rise="Hattusili I unified central Anatolia around 1650 BCE; Mursili I sacked Babylon in 1595 BCE; Suppiluliuma I made the Hittites a superpower rivalling Egypt.",fall="Around 1180 BCE drought, famine, attacks by the Kaska and Sea Peoples and internal strife led to the abandonment of Hattusa during the Bronze Age collapse.",legacy="The Treaty of Kadesh (~1259 BCE) with Ramesses II is the oldest known peace treaty; Hittite is the oldest written Indo-European language."),
"phoenicia":dict(government="Independent city-states such as Tyre, Sidon and Byblos were ruled by kings with powerful merchant councils.",architecture="Harbour cities with breakwaters and dry docks; Carthage's circular military harbour (cothon).",technology="The 22-letter consonantal alphabet (~1050 BCE), Tyrian purple dye made from murex snails, glass production, and navigation by the Pole Star that took them past Gibraltar and possibly around Africa (Herodotus).",rise="After the Bronze Age collapse weakened Egypt and the Hittites, the Phoenician cities filled the trade vacuum from about 1200 BCE and founded colonies across the Mediterranean.",fall="They fell under Assyrian, Babylonian and Persian domination; Alexander destroyed Tyre in 332 BCE after a seven-month siege.",legacy="Their alphabet is the ancestor of Greek, Latin, Hebrew, Arabic and possibly Brahmi scripts."),
"ancient-israel-and-judah":dict(government="Early Israelites were tribes led by judges; according to the Bible, Saul, David and Solomon ruled a united monarchy (~1050–930 BCE), which split into Israel in the north and Judah in the south. Prophets criticised kings for injustice.",architecture="Jerusalem's First Temple (traditionally Solomon's), Hezekiah's water tunnel (~700 BCE) and fortified cities like Megiddo and Lachish.",technology="Terrace farming of hill country, water cisterns, and early Hebrew alphabetic writing.",rise="Hill-country villages emerged around 1200 BCE; the kingdoms formed around the 10th–9th centuries BCE.",fall="Assyria destroyed Israel in 722 BCE and deported its people; Babylon destroyed Judah and the Temple in 587 BCE and exiled its elite to Babylon.",legacy="The Hebrew Bible, ethical monotheism, the Sabbath and prophetic ideas of justice shaped Judaism, Christianity and Islam."),
"kingdom-of-kush":dict(government="Kushite kings were chosen from the royal family with priestly approval; queen mothers and ruling queens (kandakes), such as Amanirenas who fought Rome, held great power.",architecture="Over 200 steep pyramids at Meroë and Nuri, temples at Jebel Barkal and Naqa.",technology="Meroë was a major iron-smelting centre with slag heaps still visible; Meroitic script (still only partly understood).",rise="Kush grew south of Egypt and, under Piye, conquered Egypt around 744 BCE, ruling as the 25th dynasty.",fall="Assyrians drove them out of Egypt (~656 BCE); Kush continued at Meroë until Aksum conquered it around 350 CE.",legacy="One of Africa's great early states; Sudan has more pyramids than Egypt."),
"carthage":dict(government="A republic led by two annually elected suffetes, a council of elders and a popular assembly; Aristotle praised its constitution.",architecture="A circular naval harbour sheltering ~220 warships and tall housing on Byrsa hill.",technology="Shipbuilding with prefabricated, numbered parts, and agriculture described by Mago.",rise="Founded by Phoenicians from Tyre around 814 BCE, it built a trading empire across North Africa, Spain, Sardinia and Sicily.",fall="It lost the three Punic Wars to Rome; in 146 BCE Rome destroyed the city.",legacy="Hannibal's campaign remains a classic of military strategy."),
"achaemenid-persian-empire":dict(government="The 'King of Kings' ruled about 20 satrapies through satraps watched by royal inspectors ('the king's eyes and ears'). Darius I standardised taxes, coinage and laws, while respecting local religions and customs.",architecture="Persepolis with its Apadana audience hall of 72 columns and reliefs of tribute bearers; Pasargadae and Cyrus's tomb.",technology="The 2,700 km Royal Road with relay stations, qanat underground canals, the gold daric coin, and a canal linking the Nile and the Red Sea.",rise="Cyrus the Great overthrew the Medes (550 BCE), conquered Lydia and Babylon (539 BCE), and his successors took Egypt and northwest India.",fall="Costly wars with Greece, palace intrigue and revolts weakened it; Alexander defeated Darius III at Issus and Gaugamela (331 BCE).",legacy="A model of tolerant, multi-ethnic empire; the Cyrus Cylinder; Persian administrative practices adopted by later empires."),
"aksum":dict(government="Aksumite 'kings of kings' ruled vassal states in Ethiopia, Eritrea and at times South Arabia.",architecture="Monolithic stelae up to 33 m tall; one was taken by Italy in 1937 and returned in 2005.",technology="Own gold, silver and bronze coins; terraced farming; Ge'ez script with vowels.",rise="Control of Red Sea trade between Rome, India and Africa made Aksum rich from the 1st century CE.",fall="Islamic control of Red Sea trade, soil exhaustion and climate change led to decline by the 10th century.",legacy="Ethiopian Christianity (from King Ezana, ~330s) and Ge'ez."),
"ghana-empire":dict(government="The king (ghana) ruled from Koumbi Saleh and taxed gold and salt trade; succession passed through the king's sister's son.",architecture="A twin-town capital with a Muslim merchant quarter of stone houses and the royal town.",technology="Organised trans-Saharan caravan trade with camels.",rise="Soninke kings controlled trade between Saharan salt and gold fields of Bambuk from around 300 CE.",fall="Almoravid pressure, drought and shifting trade routes led to decline by 1200.",legacy="West Africa's first great empire; the modern nation of Ghana took its name."),
"mali-empire":dict(government="The mansa ruled with a council and provincial governors; the Kurukan Fuga charter (1236) traditionally organised clans and rights.",architecture="Mud-brick mosques such as Djinguereber in Timbuktu, designed by the architect Es-Saheli for Mansa Musa.",technology="Manuscript libraries with works on astronomy, medicine and law.",rise="Sundiata Keita defeated the Sosso king Sumanguru at Kirina (1235).",fall="Succession disputes, Tuareg raids and the rise of Songhai.",legacy="Timbuktu's scholarship; the Sundiata epic."),
"songhai-empire":dict(government="Askia Muhammad organised provinces, a professional army and Islamic courts.",architecture="The Tomb of Askia in Gao (UNESCO).",technology="A river fleet on the Niger.",rise="Sonni Ali (r. 1464–1492) captured Timbuktu and Djenné.",fall="Moroccan musketeers defeated Songhai at Tondibi in 1591.",legacy="Largest empire in West African history."),
"great-zimbabwe":dict(government="Kings ruled from the Hill Complex; a population of perhaps 10,000–18,000 lived around the stone enclosures.",architecture="Walls up to 11 m high built of granite blocks without mortar, including the Great Enclosure and its conical tower.",technology="Gold mining and trade with the Swahili coast.",rise="Cattle wealth and gold trade from the 11th century.",fall="Overgrazing, depleted resources and shifting trade routes led to abandonment around 1450.",legacy="Colonial authorities long denied Africans built it; it became a symbol of Zimbabwe's independence."),
"swahili-city-states":dict(government="Each town had its own sultan and merchant elite.",architecture="Coral-stone mosques and palaces such as Husuni Kubwa at Kilwa.",technology="Dhows sailing with the monsoon to Arabia, India and China.",rise="Bantu coastal communities trading with Arab and Persian merchants from the 8th century.",fall="Portuguese attacks and the shift of trade after 1500.",legacy="The Swahili language, now spoken by about 200 million."),
"kingdom-of-benin":dict(government="The Oba ruled with chiefs and guilds of artisans.",architecture="Earthworks around Benin City estimated as among the largest built by people before the mechanical era.",technology="Brass and bronze casting.",rise="Emerged in the forests of southern Nigeria around the 12th century; expanded under Oba Ewuare (~1440).",fall="A British punitive expedition burned the city in 1897.",legacy="The Benin Bronzes, now being returned."),
"ottoman-empire":dict(government="The sultan ruled with a grand vizier and the Imperial Council; the devshirme system recruited Christian boys to become janissaries and officials; non-Muslim communities (millets) governed their own religious affairs.",architecture="Mimar Sinan's Süleymaniye and Selimiye mosques, Topkapı Palace and the Blue Mosque.",technology="Large gunpowder artillery (the cannons that breached Constantinople), cartography (Piri Reis's map) and an efficient postal system.",rise="Osman I's frontier principality in Anatolia expanded into the Balkans; Mehmed II took Constantinople in 1453; Selim I took Egypt and Arabia; Suleiman reached Vienna (1529).",fall="Military defeats from 1683, nationalism in the Balkans, economic dependence on Europe and defeat in World War I led to its dissolution and the Turkish Republic (1923).",legacy="A multi-faith empire lasting six centuries; modern Middle Eastern and Balkan borders took shape from its collapse."),
"safavid-iran":dict(government="Shahs ruled with Qizilbash tribal elites; Shah Abbas I built a slave-soldier army to curb them.",architecture="Isfahan's Naqsh-e Jahan square, the Shah Mosque and the Si-o-se-pol bridge.",technology="Silk and carpet industries organised for export.",rise="Shah Ismail I founded the dynasty in 1501, making Shia Islam the state religion.",fall="Weak rulers and the Afghan sack of Isfahan in 1722.",legacy="Iran's Shia identity and Persian art."),
}
